history in the making quite literally  these are the first coins featuring King  Charles III and these 50 pence pieces  also pay a special tribute to his mother  the reverse is a replica of the coin  produced to celebrate her coronation  back in 1953. So anyone under 50 will  only have ever seen Queen Elizabeth II  on the coin so significant moment today  where we're putting a new monarch into  that circulation so it's quite common  for male Monarch not to wear a crown on  their coinage it happened to his father  his grandfather well they were on  coinage there was no crowd if you're  particularly observant you'll notice  that his majesty is facing the opposite  direction to Queen Elizabeth II on the  coins that we currently use that is in  keeping with tradition when there's a  new monarch the portrait was designed by  sculptor Martin Jennings using photos  and was personally approved by the king  it has to be an image that will persist  over the years and that will represent  the generality of what people imagine  monarchy to be about so it's not just a  portrait of him it's also it needs to  contain the role we'll still have coins  with the Effigy of Queen Elizabeth II  they'll only be replaced once they're  worn or damaged but you won't have to  wait long for these which could start  appearing in your change in December  Becky Cottrell Sky News the Royal Mint
 

